        "type": "thinking",
        "thinking": "I want to plan the design carefully before writing the full file in one pass without testing: a lava golem around 2.6 meters tall in Genshin's cel-shaded style, with dark purple-grey basalt plates, glowing molten orange seams, ornate gold filigree trim, gold wrist rings, and a glowing pyro-core chest piece.\n\nI'm setting up imports for the builder, math, paint, and texture kits, then sketching the function signature and part-placement API I'll use to assemble the golem with stubby legs, a massive torso, low-hanging arms, and a small embedded head, using faceted low-poly geometry for rock plates and flat glowing colors for the molten seams.\n\nStill writing coordinates...\n\nFinishing the gauntlet and fist geometry, confirming nothing clips the floor since the feet remain the lowest points. Now I'm adding back details like lava vent spikes and glowing crust ridges along the spine, plus a seam helper function that sweeps glowing orange polylines along limb edges to accent the joints.\n\nI'm deciding to skip a layered yellow inner core for the seams since it would z-fight with the orange glow, keeping just two seam colors instead. Then I'm adding eyes as small glowing yellow spheres, a jagged glowing mouth, gold torus trims at the wrists, and designing a gold-and-orange pyro emblem decal for the chest plate using an SVG with circles and diamond strokes.\n\nNow I'm working out exact placement math for that chest emblem plane, trying to compute the frustum box's depth at the given y-height so the plate sits flush against the chest surface without floating.\n\nGiven the orientation uncertainty, I'll switch to explicit stacked BoxGeometry sections for the torso instead—a hips box, a chest box, and separate limb segments—since explicit box dimensions are more reliable than trying to interpolate frustum cross-sections.\n\nAdding pec plates and emblem placement...\n\nFor textures, I'll add a second basalt variant using scales for plates and mottle for rocks, with paint working fine on both box UVs and procedural geometry. For the spine glow, I'm placing spike parts along the back leaning backward, using the spike helper with a normalized direction vector, basalt-colored base and glowing tips.\n\nI'm adding small magma crystal spikes at the shoulder seams with orange glow cores, plus a flame crest on the head using frame cards imported from the frame module, arranged in an arc with a flame SVG texture in orange and yellow.\n\nNow I'm building the shoulder pauldrons as stacked icosahedron plates with gold torus rims, setting up helper functions for rocks and boxes with position/scale/rotation, and adding a gold belt ring plus hanging basalt loincloth slabs at the hips with glowing seams, while making sure the feet and shin geometry line up so the bottom sits flush at y=0.\n\nWorking out shin, knee, thigh, hip, and abdomen box placements...\n\nNow I'm placing seam cracks on the torso front and back, then moving to the arms — rod geometry for upper arm and forearm with elbow glow rings, down to a fist rock at the wrist.\n\nContinuing to position elbow and bracer details, then shaping the head: a rock base scaled to a blocky form, a dark brow ridge box, and slanted glowing eye boxes near the top.\n\nAdding a small orange icosahedron at the base plus glowing crack rods along the back, skipping ground lava and floating embers for simplicity. For colors, I'm defining the eye glow as warm yellow, the seams in orange and hot tones, and gold via a cel-shaded gradient. For the emblem, I'm designing an SVG with a gold-stroked circle, inner diamond, and flame motif in orange and pale yellow against a dark glowing background.\n\nWriting SVG code...\n\nStill shaping shoulder armor...\n\nChecking the sweep helper signature and confirming it works for zigzag seams with square cross-sections, and making sure glow accepts swept meshes. I'm also planning to scatter small debris rocks around the character's feet using rng-seeded positions so the ground looks natural.\n\n",
